  "scenes": [
    {
      "id": "hook",
      "narration": "What do a bathtub, a lemon, and a race car have in common? You can understand all three with one simple idea... the integral.",
      "actions": [
        {
          "type": "drawing",
          "drawing": "Bathtub",
          "at": "bathtub",
          "duration": 1.0,
          "x": 210,
          "y": 430,
          "width": 290,
          "height": 240
        },
        {
          "type": "drawing",
          "drawing": "Lemon",
          "at": "lemon",
          "duration": 0.9,
          "x": 540,
          "y": 430,
          "width": 270,
          "height": 200
        },
        {
          "type": "drawing",
          "drawing": "Car",
          "at": "car",
          "duration": 1.0,
          "x": 870,
          "y": 440,
          "width": 300,
          "height": 140
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "all three = *one idea*",
          "at": "understand",
          "x": 540,
          "y": 700,
          "size": 96
        },
        {
          "type": "drawing",
          "drawing": "IntegralSign",
          "at": "integral",
          "duration": 1.0,
          "x": 540,
          "y": 1060,
          "width": 200,
          "height": 440
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "*INTEGRALS*",
          "at": "integral",
          "delay": 0.6,
          "x": 540,
          "y": 1390,
          "size": 150,
          "cps": 16
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "tub",
      "narration": "Let's start with a bathtub. You open the tap. Sometimes the water flows fast... sometimes slowly. Every second, a thin layer of water is added to the tub. How much water is in there at the end? Just add up all the thin layers! That's what an integral does: it adds up lots of tiny pieces.",
      "actions": [
        {
          "type": "3d",
          "model": "Tank3D",
          "at": "bathtub",
          "duration": 1.6,
          "then": "tap",
          "thenDuration": 12.0,
          "cues": [
            "add"
          ],
          "cueDuration": 1.0,
          "x": 540,
          "y": 760,
          "width": 1040,
          "height": 960
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "fast!",
          "at": "fast",
          "x": 880,
          "y": 300,
          "size": 90,
          "color": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "slow...",
          "at": "slowly",
          "x": 880,
          "y": 390,
          "size": 80,
          "color": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "1 second = *1 thin layer*",
          "at": "every",
          "x": 540,
          "y": 1290,
          "size": 86
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "total = *sum of all layers*",
          "at": "add",
          "x": 540,
          "y": 1400,
          "size": 86
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "integral = adding up *tiny pieces*",
          "at": "integral",
          "x": 540,
          "y": 1510,
          "size": 78
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "lemon",
      "narration": "Now, a lemon. How much lemon is in there? It's a curvy shape, so it's hard to measure. So let's slice it! Each slice is almost a flat coin, and coins are easy to measure. Add up all the coins, and you get the whole lemon. Thinner slices? An even more exact answer.",
      "actions": [
        {
          "type": "3d",
          "model": "Lemon3D",
          "at": "lemon",
          "duration": 1.4,
          "cues": [
            "slice",
            "thinner"
          ],
          "cueDuration": 1.4,
          "x": 540,
          "y": 780,
          "width": 1040,
          "height": 820
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "curvy → *hard to measure*",
          "at": "curvy",
          "x": 540,
          "y": 300,
          "size": 86
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "1 slice ≈ *1 coin*",
          "at": "coin",
          "x": 540,
          "y": 1270,
          "size": 90
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "lemon = coin + coin + coin + …",
          "at": "add",
          "x": 540,
          "y": 1380,
          "size": 74
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "thinner slices → *more exact*",
          "at": "thinner",
          "x": 540,
          "y": 1490,
          "size": 82
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "bridge",
      "narration": "Okay! Now let's draw it on a graph, like a real mathematician.",
      "actions": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "On a graph",
          "at": "okay",
          "x": 540,
          "y": 760,
          "size": 170,
          "cps": 14
        },
        {
          "type": "drawing",
          "drawing": "Underline",
          "props": {
            "count": 2
          },
          "at": "graph",
          "duration": 0.8,
          "x": 540,
          "y": 880,
          "width": 640,
          "height": 90,
          "color": "yellow"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "like a real *mathematician*",
          "at": "like",
          "x": 540,
          "y": 1060,
          "size": 90
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "graph",
      "narration": "This is a race car's speed during a race. Here's the cool part: the distance it travels is the area under this curve. But the curve is wobbly... so we fill it with rectangles. A rectangle is easy: width times height. With four rectangles, it's a rough guess. With ten, much closer. With a hundred super thin ones... almost perfect. Make them infinitely thin, and the guess becomes exact. That's an integral!",
      "actions": [
        {
          "type": "drawing",
          "drawing": "Car",
          "at": "car",
          "duration": 0.9,
          "x": 190,
          "y": 280,
          "width": 250,
          "height": 120
        },
        {
          "type": "drawing",
          "drawing": "RiemannGraph",
          "at": "speed",
          "duration": 2.2,
          "cues": [
            "area",
            "rectangles",
            "ten",
            "hundred",
            "exact"
          ],
          "cueDuration": 1.2,
          "x": 540,
          "y": 800,
          "width": 1000,
          "height": 730
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "area = *distance*",
          "at": "distance",
          "x": 690,
          "y": 290,
          "size": 96
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "rectangle = width × height",
          "at": "width",
          "x": 540,
          "y": 1270,
          "size": 80
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "infinitely thin → *EXACT*",
          "at": "infinitely",
          "x": 540,
          "y": 1390,
          "size": 94
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "= *integral*",
          "at": "that's",
          "x": 540,
          "y": 1510,
          "size": 100
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "notation",
      "narration": "Mathematicians write it like this. This long S stands for sum. f of x is the height of each rectangle. d x is its tiny width. And a and b tell you where to start, and where to stop.",
